{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by ben jesson and gran heller. the film is based on the true story of the founding of mark zuckerberg and the creation of facebook.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, a harvard college student who creates a social networking site called facebook while attending college. the film also features a talented ensemble cast, including andrew garfield as dustin moskovitz, james franco as edgar ferguson, and armie hammer as cambria, zuckerberg's rival for friendship and love.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its portrayal of the complex and often tumultuous relationship between zuckerberg and his friends and colleagues. the film does an excellent job of capturing the intensity and ambition of the characters, and the way they are driven to succeed, even if it means sacrificing their relationships and integrity.\n\nthe film also explores the themes of friendship, betrayal, and the consequences of success. the characters are multidimensional and relatable, and the film does an excellent job of conveying the complexity and nuance of the characters' motivations and emotions.\n\nthe technical aspects of the film are also noteworthy. the cinematography is sleek and modern, capturing the vibrant colors and energy of harvard college life. the editing is quick and efficient, moving the story along at a pace that is both engaging and suspenseful.\n\nthe score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and evocative, perfectly capturing the mood and atmosphere of the film. the sound design is also top-notch, with a haunting and atmospheric soundtrack that adds to the overall mood of the film.\n\noverall, the social network is a powerful and thought-provoking film that explores the complexities of friendship, ambition, and the consequences o